(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-intro-anatomy-quiz"]={"title":"Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology — Anatomy Exam 1","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Systemic anatomy organizes the body according to what?","choices":["Discrete body regions (head/neck, thorax, pelvis)","Functional systems (integumentary, musculoskeletal, nervous, etc.)","The embryological origin of the tissue exclusively, without regard to adult location","Clinical practice specialties alone, without regard to body structure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Systemic anatomy is organized according to functional systems: integumentary, musculoskeletal, nervous, circulatory, respiratory, digestive, urinary, reproductive, and endocrine. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Regional anatomy is concerned with what?","choices":["Only the skeletal system across the whole body","All systems found in a discrete part of the body","Only the processes of clinical disease","Only the nervous system, whole body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Regional anatomy is concerned with all systems found in a discrete part of the body (head and neck, back, thorax and abdomen, pelvis, upper and lower extremities), with emphasis on relationships of systemic structures within that region. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"In the anatomical position, how are the upper limbs and palms oriented?","choices":["By the sides with palms facing posteriorly","Crossed over the chest with palms facing posteriorly","Raised overhead with palms facing medially","By the sides with palms facing anteriorly"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the anatomical position, the upper limbs are by the sides with the palms facing anteriorly. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why are all anatomical descriptions expressed relative to the anatomical position?","choices":["Because it is the most comfortable position to stand in for long periods","To ensure descriptions are not ambiguous, regardless of the person's actual position","Because it is required for radiographic imaging positioning only","Because it is a position used solely in the context of surgical procedures"],"answer":1,"exp":"All anatomical descriptions are expressed in relation to the anatomical position to ensure the descriptions are not ambiguous, regardless of the actual position a person may be in. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does the median (mid) sagittal plane do?","choices":["Passes in a horizontal orientation through the body, separating it into superior and inferior portions","Passes vertically through the center of the body, dividing it into right and left sections","Divides the body into upper and lower limb sections only","Passes in a vertical orientation through the body, separating it into anterior and posterior portions"],"answer":1,"exp":"The median (mid) sagittal plane is a vertical plane through the center of the body that divides it into right and left sections. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does the coronal (frontal) plane do, and how does it relate to the median plane?","choices":["Separates the body into anterior and posterior portions, running parallel to the median plane","Separates the body into right and left portions, running parallel to the median plane","Separates the body into superior and inferior portions, at a right angle to the median plane","Divides the body into anterior and posterior sections, at a right angle to the median plane"],"answer":3,"exp":"The coronal (frontal) plane is a vertical plane that divides the body into anterior and posterior sections, at a right angle to the median plane. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does the transverse plane do?","choices":["A vertical plane dividing the body into right and left sections","A horizontal plane dividing the body into superior and inferior sections","A vertical plane dividing the body into anterior and posterior sections","A horizontal plane dividing the body into anterior and posterior sections"],"answer":1,"exp":"The transverse plane is a horizontal plane passing through the body, dividing it into superior and inferior sections. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What do the terms \"proximal\" and \"distal\" describe?","choices":["Proximal and distal both describe left and right laterality of a structure","Proximal describes what is closest to the outer body surface; distal describes what is farthest from that surface","Proximal means anterior on the body; distal means posterior on the body","Proximal is closest to a point of reference (the body's center, in general use); distal is farthest from it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Proximal describes an area closest to a point of reference (generally, closest to the body's center), while distal describes an area farthest from that point of reference. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What do the terms \"superficial\" and \"deep\" describe?","choices":["Superficial and deep both describe movement at a joint","Superficial is closest to the body's center; deep is farthest from the center","Superficial means anterior; deep means posterior","Superficial is closest to the body surface; deep is farthest from the surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"Superficial describes a point closest to the surface of the body, while deep describes a point farthest from the surface. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the difference between \"internal\" and \"external\" as anatomical terms?","choices":["They describe a structure's vertical superior/inferior position relative to another","They describe closest to/farthest from the surface","They describe a structure's anterior/posterior position relative to another","They describe the distance of a structure from the center of an organ"],"answer":3,"exp":"Internal and external describe the distance of a structure from the center of an organ. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What do \"supine\" and \"prone\" describe?","choices":["Supine describes the hand's palm; prone describes the foot's sole","Supine describes lying face down; prone describes lying on one's back","Supine and prone are both terms used to describe upright standing postures","Supine is lying on the back; prone is lying face down on the ventral surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"Supine means lying on the back, while prone means lying on the ventral surface (face down). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does \"rostral\" mean, and in what context is it often used?","choices":["Situated toward the bottom end of the body, a term often applied when describing the limbs","Situated at the body's midline, used only in embryology and development","Situated on the lateral surface, used in orthopedics and joint description","Situated near the front of the body (Latin for \"beak\"), often used in neurological terms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rostral means situated near the front of the body (Latin for \"beak\"), sometimes used interchangeably with anterior, and is often used in neurological terms. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does \"caudal\" mean, and in what context is it often used?","choices":["Situated near the bottom or end of an organism (Latin for \"tail\"), often used in neurological/spinal terms","Situated superiorly, used exclusively in skull and cranial terminology","Situated toward the front of the body, sometimes used interchangeably with the term anterior","Situated on the medial surface, used specifically for the hand and wrist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Caudal means situated near the bottom or end of an organism (Latin for \"tail\"), sometimes used interchangeably with posterior, and is often used in neurological terms related to the spine. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does \"cephalic\" mean?","choices":["Toward the head (Greek for \"head\"), sometimes used interchangeably with superior","Directed toward the back surface of the body, in the dorsal direction","Toward the midline of the body, from the Latin for \"middle\"","Directed toward the feet, a term sometimes used interchangeably with inferior"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cephalic means toward the head (Greek for \"head\"), sometimes used interchangeably with superior, and is often used in neurology. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does \"ventral\" denote, and when is it typically used?","choices":["Superior to another structure; typically used when describing skull sutures and cranial landmarks specifically","Medial to another structure; typically used when describing midline trunk structures specifically","Anterior to another structure; typically used when a structure divides into two parts with an opposite dorsal structure (e.g., ventral horn vs. dorsal horn)","Posterior to another structure; typically used when describing paired limb landmarks specifically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ventral denotes something anterior to another structure, typically used when the anatomical structure is easily divided into two parts with an opposite dorsal structure, such as the ventral horn versus dorsal horn. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does \"palmar\" describe?","choices":["The superior surface of a protruding body part","The inferior surface of the foot","The anterior surface of the hand","The posterior surface of the hand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Palmar is used to describe the \"anterior\" surface of the hand. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does \"plantar\" describe?","choices":["The posterior surface of the hand, called the dorsum","The inferior surface of the foot, not visible when standing","The anterior surface of the hand, called the palm","The superior surface of the foot, the dorsum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Plantar describes the \"inferior\" surface of the foot — the surface that is not visible when standing. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does \"dorsal\" describe when referring to a protruding body part, and what are some examples given?","choices":["The anterior surface of the hand only, called the palmar surface of the palm","The superior/back surface of a part that protrudes anterior from the body — e.g., dorsum of the foot, hand, penis, or tongue","The medial surface of the trunk, located close to the body's vertical midline","The inferior surface of the foot only, called the plantar surface of the sole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dorsal describes the superior or dorsal (back) surface of any part that protrudes anterior from the body, with examples including the dorsum of the foot, the dorsum of the hand, the dorsum of the penis, and the dorsum of the tongue. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does \"inferomedial\" mean, and what is the example given?","choices":["Situated nearer to the head and farther away from the median plane, as with the superolateral brain surface","Nearer to the feet and farther from the median plane; e.g., the inferolateral rib margin","Nearer to the feet and closer to the median plane; e.g., the anterior ribs are directed inferomedially","Nearer to the head and closer to the median plane; e.g., the superomedial orbit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inferomedial means nearer to the feet and closer to the median plane; the example given is that the anterior parts of the ribs are directed inferomedially. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the difference between \"bilateral\" and \"unilateral\" structures, with examples?","choices":["Paired structures (kidneys, lungs, extremities) are bilateral; single-sided structures (spleen, liver) are unilateral","Bilateral is used to describe structures present on only one side; unilateral describes paired structures","Both terms are said to describe the exact same thing: structures present on both sides","Bilateral and unilateral both refer only to structures of the upper and lower limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Paired structures such as the kidneys, lungs, and extremities are bilateral, left, or right, while structures found on only one side, such as the spleen and liver, are unilateral. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What do \"ipsilateral\" and \"contralateral\" denote?","choices":["Ipsilateral denotes the opposite side; contralateral denotes the same side","Both terms describe superior/inferior relationships","Ipsilateral denotes the same side; contralateral denotes the opposite side","Both terms describe anterior/posterior relationships"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ipsilateral denotes (of) the same side, while contralateral denotes (on) the opposite side. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"According to the material, most anatomical descriptions of position are expressed relative to what reference?","choices":["The anatomical position","The position used only in radiographic imaging","An arbitrary reference chosen per description","The position the person happens to be in at the time"],"answer":0,"exp":"All anatomical descriptions are expressed in relation to the anatomical position, regardless of the actual position a person may be in. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the anatomical position, how are the lower limbs, feet, and toes oriented?","choices":["Positioned with the feet spread apart and turned outward laterally","Positioned close together, with the toes directed toward the back of the body","Close together with the feet parallel and toes directed anteriorly","Crossed at the ankles with toes turned medially"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the anatomical position, the lower limbs are close together with the feet parallel and the toes directed anteriorly. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the anatomical position, where are the head, eyes, and toes directed?","choices":["Laterally (outward)","Posterior (backward)","Anterior (forward)","Superior (upward)"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the anatomical position, the head, eyes, and toes are directed anterior (forward). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which term describes the intermediate position \"nearer to the head and farther from the median plane,\" with the superolateral surface of the brain as the given example?","choices":["Superolateral","Inferolateral","Inferomedial","Superomedial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Superolateral means nearer to the head and farther from the median plane; the example given is the superolateral surface of the brain. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does clinical anatomy emphasize?","choices":["Only the technique of cadaveric dissection, without regard to living patients","Structure and function as it relates to the practice of medicine","Only embryological development of the tissues","Only the classification scheme used for the organ systems of the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Clinical anatomy emphasizes structure and function as it relates to the practice of medicine. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which pair of terms describes an area's distance from a point of reference, most often the body's center in general use?","choices":["Proximal and distal","Superficial and deep","Ipsilateral and contralateral","Rostral and caudal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Proximal (closest to a point of reference) and distal (farthest from a point of reference) describe distance from a reference point, generally the body's center. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which term pair is often used interchangeably with anterior/posterior, respectively, particularly in neurological contexts?","choices":["Superficial and deep","Proximal and distal","Rostral and caudal","Palmar and plantar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rostral is sometimes used interchangeably with anterior, and caudal is sometimes used interchangeably with posterior, particularly in neurological terms. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as an example of a regional anatomy division?","choices":["The nervous system","The circulatory system","The endocrine system","Thorax and abdomen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thorax and abdomen is one of the regional divisions listed (alongside head and neck, back, pelvis, and upper and lower extremities). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which term describes lying on the ventral surface, face down?","choices":["Prone","Caudal","Supine","Rostral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Prone means lying on the ventral surface (face down). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"}]};
